The expressive power of templates
=================================

The current state of things is:

- interfaces are sets of method prototypes. A class is said to comply with an interface if it implements all the interface's methods.
- declaring an interface also creates a so called "generic" class. This generic class cannot be instantiated, but objects of compliant classes can be casted to it. This generic class can be used as any other class: methods can be written for it (via `extend`), variables can use it as their type, it can be passed as argument, etc. Behind the scene, it uses the virtual table mechanism: when an object is casted to it, pointers to its relevants methods are attached to it, which are then used to make the calls.

Generic classes are great to write algorithms that apply to similar classes, in a concise and elegant way: any object compliant to the interface can be casted - often implicitely - and used right away.

The cons are that virtual calls are slightly slower, and that generics are completely useless to store objects: no downcasting is possible, so an object casted to a generic loses all its properties outside of these defined by the interface. In particular, general purpose containers cannot be written using generics. Even if downcasting was allowed, containers would be very unefficient: the user would need to remember what class is contained in a particular container, and (dynamically) downcast each element before every use. Unacceptable. Furthermore, because of the virtual table mechanism, generics work only with objects. Values need to be boxed before they can be casted to generics.

Therefore, for containers (and algorithms that cannot afford the loss of efficiency due to virtual calls), we also need templates.

Since the main use is for containers, I decided that only full classes can be templated. Individual methods would be too bothersome, and if really required, they can always be wrapped in classes anyway. So:

- classes can be declared to take template parameters. These parameters are resolved only when the template is "instantiated", that is used with concrete classes. The entire class is then rewritten by the compiler, for these particular classes, and all types and calls can be early bound.

This is how templates work in most languages. Now I wanted to add some fancyness:

- the class parameters to a template are bounded by interfaces. That is, the template declares, for each class parameter, an interface to which the concrete type must comply when instantiating.

This was to simplify analysis: by restricting the set of methods a parameter class can call, you only need to analyze the code in the template once. Then at instantiation, simply verify that the given class complies to the interface used as bound.

This sounded great: simplified analysis, and some form of theoretical niceness. A class template is some kind of function, which takes in class parameters, and returns a class. These parameters have a sort of "type" which restricts them:

	object Farm|(Animal A) end
	
`Farm|Pig` is ok, `Farm|Car` is not, because we will most likely try to feed and breed and slaughter the animals. That won't work on cars.

	object List|(Any T) end

Anything is ok in a container, because we only move them around, and assignment and copy are operations that are built-in for all classes.

This restriction caused by the interface seems completely alright, because it prevents you to try an instantiate a template that would fail anyway. In C++, templates don't have these bounds, and you can always try to use them for anything. But within the template's code, if you call a method that the type doesn't support, it will fail. Having bounds seems to be a great way to catch the error early, for an equivalent result.

Unfortunately, it is not that simple. The reality is that the expressive power of this system is far lesser. Example.

	template <class T>
	T add(T a, T b)
	{
		return a + b;
	}
	
	// later
	
	int i = add<int>(4,10);		// or even "add(4,10)"

Simple enough. In sool, we can only template classes, but we can always make dummy ones and selfless methods, that not the problem. Let's try:

	object Adder|(MyBound T)
		selfless T add(T a, T b)
			return a + b
		end
	end

Seems easy as well. Now we just have to define the bound. Our class T up there only need to answer to the `+` operator:

	interface Addable
		Addable +(Addable)
	end

Can you see the problem? This interface is defined recursively. `Addable` here as argument and return type is the generic type, which means "any class that complies to Addable". Except that no class can, because it would need to define a method that uses Addables, not itself! And there is no such thing as Addables in the first place.

OK, lets use concrete types, by using an interface template as well:

	interface Addable|(Any T)
		T +(T)
	end

which represents "you can add a T to it, and it returns a T". For instance, `num` complies to `Addable|num`. So far so good. The problem is when we put this back in the Adder:

	object Adder|(Addable|T T)
		--
	end

The interface `Addable|T` does not exist, because `T` is not a class... `T` doesn't exist yet. We could decide to allow a parameter's bound to depend on the parameter (which sounds completely absurd)

	local sum = Adder|num:add(3,10)

But then can other bounds depend on previous parameters?

	class Foo|(Any T, Interf|T U, Other|U V) end

Technically it seems feasible, since instantiation is always done by giving all parameters first. The bounds could be deduced from these, and then compliance checking could occur. But how weird and unnatural is that... And having to define a num as an Addable|num? Hmm.

Furthermore I realized just by writing the interface earlier that a bound to an interface template is useless: no code is called by the interface, only prototypes are defined. The restriction would be purely arbitrary, and you could use `Any` all the time.

Soooo, if it's useless on an interface, and too much constraint on a class, maybe template parameter bounds have no reason to exist?

Hmm. Let's see. D, like C++, has "completely free" template parameters. Rust has the same system as currently described for sool: you have to give an interface to define what operations can do your parameter. If you omit it, it is assumed that it can do nothing (only be moved around).

How does Rust survive with this? Well, this system, albeit very constraining, is enough to define containers. Containers don't do anything with their containees: they just store them, and retrieve them when needed. And containers was the one thing we needed templates for.

What about "efficient" algorithms? For instance, once we have a vector, or list of T, we might want to sort it, whatever T is. That would mean having to impose an additional restriction on T, the fact that it can be compared to another T, and we have the same recursive interface definition problem:

	-- not what we want:
	interface Comparable
		bool <(Comparable)
	end
	
	-- not exactly what we want:
	interface Comparable|(Any T)
		bool <(T)
	end

Then you'd have num comply to Comparable|num, but any class you try to define for sortable container would fail the same:

	class SortableVector|(Comparable|T T) end	-- eeek

And yet, Rust has templates for sorting facilities and such. How do they do that? Well, they don't impose the restriction on the parameter itself. They just ask you to provide the function that actually compares elements (Rust has first level functions). In sool, the equivalent would be a dummy object that carries the function with it (a "functor"):

	interface Comparer|(Any T)
		bool compare(T,T)
	end

	class Sorter|(Any T)
		method sort(Vector|T vec, Comparer|T comp)
			-- use comp.compare(a,b)
		end
	end
	
Still not perfect. `Comparer|T` is a generic type, and would require casting and virtual calls to `compare`. In the end, we have the equivalent of a function pointer. Which is what happens in Rust when you pass a function to the sorting algorithm.

Can't we statically bind this at all? We would like to be able to do:

	class Sorter|(Any T, Comparer|T Comp)
		method sort(Vector|T vec, Comp c)
			-- use Comp:compare(), which known at compile time
		end
	end

And we're back to "can we use template parameters in the bounds of other template parameters?" That would be equivalent to having templates within templates and doing some stuff that looks like currification. Mmm, functional programming...

The problem with this approach is that since the bound depends on the parameter, you can't know it until template instantiation. And that voids our idea that the template can be validated once, using the (generic) bound as the (actual) class in the code. Which would be equivalent to having to validate the full template for each instance of it. Which then would require just as much work than having no bound at all, and makes the whole bound concept useless.

So no, bounds cannot depend on the parameters.

Functors
--------

Since I started mentioning them, I might as well continue. Functors are objects whose sole purpose are to be executed "as a function", so that we can dynamically choose between functions at runtime. Or alternatively, at compile time using templates.

For instance it can be used in a collection, to apply an operation to every element and save the result in place:

	extend #num
		method map(Functor f)
			for i,v in self do
				self[i] = f.call(v)
			end
		end
	end

Now what is the type "Functor"? Since in sool there are no first-level function, Functor has to be a class. Now of course if we wrote:

	class Functor
		method num call(num i)
			-- do something with it
		end
	end

that would be quite boring, and all instances of Functor would do exactly the same. No, the interest comes by having Functor be an interface, and describe various operations in classes that comply to it:

	interface Functor
		num call(num)
	end
	
	class Double
		method num call(num i)
			return i*2
		end
	end

	class SetToZero
		method num call(num i)
			return 0
		end
	end

Then instances of these classes can be casted to the generic type Functor, and be passed to the array's method:

	local d = Double	
	local arr = [1,2,3,4,5]
	
	arr.map(d)	-- d is casted to a Functor

Inside `map`, `f.call(v)` is now a virtual call. We could have put a SetToZero insted:

	arr.map(SetToZero)

Note that you can use an anonymous object, which makes a nice concise syntax. Also, we can use value classes for the functors, no need for an object on the heap. Especially when it doesn't have state, in which case we can also write a selfless method:

	value Decrement
		selfless num call(num i)
			return i - 1
		end
	end

With that last example, we have something that is almost exactly a function pointer. No state, the class is there only to ensure the type safety. But we can have more, by carrying state around:
	
	interface Functor2
		void call(num)
	end

	object Accumulator
		num sum = 0
		method void call(num i)
			sum = sum + i
		end
	end
	
	-- later
	
	local arr = [1,2,3,4,5]
	local acc = Accumulator
	arr.apply(acc)			-- acc is casted to a Functor2
	assert(acc.sum == 15)

By carrying state around, we can get fancy effects. Note that this time we need an object. Otherwise we wouldn't have been able to retrieve the result. Alternatively we can design the `apply` method to return the functor once it's done, in which case we could have done:

	local sum = arr.apply(acc).sum

Of course this is far heavier than having actual functions. We need to define interfaces for each and every function prototype we need, and then classes for each individual function. And as we saw before, it's not always possible to make templates for the "functions".

Some functors are quite generic and often applicable. They exist in C++'s `<algorithm>`, for instance:

	interface UnaryPredicate|T
		bool call(T)
	end
	
	interface BinaryPredicate|(T,U)
		bool call(T,U)
	end
	
	interface Function|T
		call(T)
	end
	
	interface UnaryOperator|T
		T call(T)
	end
	
	interface BinaryOperator|T
		T call(T,T)
	end
	
etc. With this you can have generic algorithms:

	extend #T
		method T find(UnaryPredicate|T pred)
			for v in self do
				if pred.call(v) then
					return v
				end
			end
		end
	end

But like we saw, it's difficult (impossible?) to write some functors generically. We could have, for instance:

	value LowerThan
		num bound
		
		selfless new(num n)
			return LowerThan(bound = n)
		end
	
		selfless bool call(num i)
			return i < bound
		end
	end
	
	-- later
	
	local arr = [10,7,11,3,20,1]
	local res = arr.find(LowerThan(5))
	-- res == 3

Pretty, isn't it? Well, to some extent. As we saw before, we can't generalize this functor to be "lower than" anything else than a num. We'd have to write it again for integers, and any other class we need. And it still involves one virtual call per iteration.

But it can still be quite useful, to avoid writing the same code over and over again, despite the limits of our template system.

Last word about performance
---------------------------

So, we saw that some algorithms were not writable using templates, only using the generic late-bound classes.

However, as we mentionned earlier, some optimizations can be done by the compiler. When calling a generic method, sometimes the compiler definitely knows the actual type that is casted to a generic:

	arr.sort(MyComparisonFunctor)

The MyComparisonFunctor instance that is passed to `sort` is in principle casted and attached to a virtual table, so that the generic `sort` can be run. But since we definitely know what actual type it is, the `sort` function could be automatically "templated", and generated for the specific class of functor. And in this instance, the functor can be resolved to early bound code, or even inlined.

And there you have it, the performance of templates with the elegance of generic types. For the price of more work in the comiler. But why not?

Selfless vs. selfish in generics
--------------------------------

A thing came to my mind while writing this about functors. When a class defines a selfless method,

	object Class
		selfless no_self()
		end
	end

 it can be called either without an object:

	Class:no_self()

or with one:

	local instance = Class
	instance.no_self()

And of course from the object's methods itself:

	extend Class
		method foo()
			no_self()
			
			-- same as:
			self.no_self()
			Class:no_self()
		end
	end

In this context, everything is statically binded, and the compiler can directly write the low-level code to the selfless method, which really looks like a function.

But lately I've been dealing with interfaces and naturally considered that if you can write:

	instance.foo()

then it really means that the object implements `void:foo:void` and therefore would comply with an interface:

	interface HasFoo
		foo()
	end

Unfortunately, the low-level calls for a selfless and a "selfish" method are different:

	instance.no_self(arg) 	-- is really Class_no_self(arg)
	instance.selfish(arg)	-- is really Class_selfish(instance,arg)

Therefore, casting to a generic HasFoo would be confusing. In the virtual table, if you put a sefless method, and the generic tries to call it as a selfish one, the ABI for arguments won't match and catastrophy ensues...

Two solutions:

- interfaces include the "selfless" information. Meaning that an object can be casted to the corresponding generic only if its methods match the selflessness defined in the interface.
- classes with selfless methods who want to be casted to generics must wrap them into dummy selfish calls.

First one is very awkward:

	interface CanFooBar
		foo()
		selfless bar()
	end

Interfaces define what messages an object must answer to. Selfless methods are just an artificial mechanism to include "classic" functions under class namespaces. Having such an interface would mean "the object answers to foo(), and the class must somehow also have a bar() function". But since `bar()` is not related to any object of the class, that makes little sense.

The second one is better. It allows classes who have a "poorer" definition of a method (without self) to still act as if they had a richer version.

	interface HasFoo
		foo()
	end

	object A
		num i
		
		method foo()
			io:print("I'm an A, and my num is ", i)
		end
	end
	
	object B
		selfless foo()
			io:print("It is I, the B!")
		end
	end

Class B doesn't hold state, and its `foo` always does the same. Defining B's `foo` as a normal method would be a useless loss of performance: in static calls, you would need an instance, and the call would always include a self parameter:

	-- if it was "method foo()" in B:
	
	local dummy = B		-- instantiation, memory use, etc...
	B.foo()
	
	-- as a selfless method:
	
	B:foo()				-- no instance, no memory, just a function call

But it would sure be nice to be able to use B as a HasFoo.

	local myB = B
	local myHasFoo = myB to HasFoo

But when casting, we build a virtual table, with a pointer to the `Foo` implementation. And class B only has a selfless `Foo`. Trying to call it with an object will fail at low-level.

Can the compiler fill-in a dummy method with a self argument for us? Would be nice. Of course, that generates more code (a forwarding function that only calls the selfless one), and the virtual call will pass a useless argument. But semantically, it works.

Which brings us back to:

Why selfless methods?
---------------------

Do we need them? We definitely don't *need* them. They have less features than normal ones, so we could always do with the normal ones. The reason for them was performance. You can always do:

	object Foo
		method bar()
			io:print("Hallo")
		end
	end
	
	-- later
	
	Foo.bar()

But this means instantiate a Foo, and call `bar` on it. That takes memory, and the call passes the pointer to the instance as argument, even though it is not used.

Now I'm not certain, but I might have decided about selfless methods before the language had structs ("value classes"). If you use a value class, there is not instantiation involved... Only some local memory is reserved (on the stack, or even in registers):

	value Foo
		method bar()
			io:print "Whatev'"
		end
	end
	
	-- later:
	
	Foo.bar()

Here, the "instantiation" doesn't create an object, just a temporary value whose address will be passed to the method (who won't use it). And temporary values' creation are very cheap: it's just space on the stack, or even just in registers. And if the "value" is actually empty, there's really no memory involved.

Plus, if the method is small enough, it will be inlined, and then when the compiler sees that the value is not even used, it will likely optimize it out. In the end, it will be equivalent.

OK, you say, there is no performance cost when using dummy empty value classes. But often, you store "functions" in classes, because they are related to the class. They won't be empty, there will be lots of things in it, initializations, maybe a complex constructor. The price of instantiation is not going to be zero.

True. But check this: how often do you call a static method from the outside of a class? If it is "related" to the class, you will often call it from inside the class (in which case you already have an object: self), or from the outside but from a "related" piece of code in which you probably manipulate an object already.

Also, small functions will be inlined and optimized. Big functions won't be inlined, and then the cost of passing a useless argument is negligible.

The only problem left is when you absolutely don't already have an object of the class and still need a "selfless" call. For instance the `new` factory:

	object Foo
		num i
		
		method new(num i)
			return Foo(i = i)
		end
	end

This `new` is normally selfless, since it's meant to build new objects with the convenience of function call syntax, and possible "complex" calculations in it.

	local foo = Foo(5)		-- is really Foo:new(5)

If we don't have selfless methods anymore, we need a real Foo before we can call `new` on it, and we're biting our own tail:

	local foo = Foo
	local foo2 = foo.new(5)

Meh? What if the class Foo has non optional references in it, that absolutely need manual initialization (in C++ terms: no default constructor)?

	local foo = Foo		-- error!

Then we'd need a helper class to hold our factory "function":

	value FooMaker
		method Foo new(num i)
			return Foo(i = i)
		end
	end
	
	-- later:
	
	local foo = FooMaker.new(5)
	-- or even, if we keep the shortcut:
	local foo = FooMaker(5)

Now ain't that ugly. That would be the price of a language without first-order functions. By putting the object at the center of the language, it does mean that to do anything, you first need either an object to do it for you, or an object to do it on. And that includes creating other objects.

Conceptually sound, but annoying. And an annoying language is not a language people want to use.

Remember, we removed the constructor-like `init` methods, because they forced us to do special semantic analysis, to be certain that no member was used before it was initialized. That turned out to be quite difficult, and forced this particular method to be treated specially.

Therefore we forced object creation to happen with the former `with` syntax:

	foo = Class(member = value, ...)

which makes things much easier: the object exists only after this call, so all information must be created prior to the call. The compiler only needs to check that all mandatory members are set. And the "created prior to" led us to have these factory functions `new`.

Conclusion: no solution is perfect. No "selfless" methods would make the language simpler, without necessarily loss of performance. But it would make some things less elegant to write.

Ah, the dilemma.

Special case: factories
-----------------------

Maybe the solution is to have no selfless methods, but to have a special case for factory functions! As we just saw, you can always do without selfless methods. Only one case would be really awkward: `new`, because we'd need helper objects.

Let's make `new` a special thing. Not a method, but more like a function, strongly associated to a class.

	object Foo
		-- lots of members
		
		new(lots of args)
			-- lots of calculations
			return Foo(setting all members in a new Foo)
		end
	end
	
	-- later
	
	local foo = Foo(args)

It would be the only thing that is written like a function in low-level (no object). It's not a real method, so you can't call `new` from the object, nor from the outside. The only way to invoke it is the object/value instantiation syntax.

For convenience, it would also be overloadable:

	object Foo
		num i
		text t
		
		new(num i, text t)
			return Foo(i = i, t = t)
		end
		
		new()
			return Foo(i = 5, t = "default")
		end
	end

In fact, most semantics would be the same as methods. But it would be the only true function. Assuming this, we could get rid of selfless and all the issues that came with it:

- syntax: no more `class:method`, so the colon can be reserved for namespaces
- simpler language: no need to decide which is selfless and which is not. Also you can't do the mistake of declaring selfless and using self, or forgetting to declare selfless in a method that doesn't use self. One less keyword.
- simpler compiler: only one kind of method. `new` functions are special, true, but they are very specific and happen at only one place.
- no ambiguity with interfaces: all methods are called the same way, so casting to generic always works (the same). `new` is not a method, and cannot be in an interface. If you need something like "make an object of the same class", write a clone method yourself.
- not so much performance drop: small methods can be inlined and the extra useless `self` argument disappears. Big methods are big, and the extra argument is negligeable.

As for the extra instantiation "just" to call a selfless method, well... If it applies to an existing object, it should probably be a method for it (and no excuses, the `extend` syntax lets you do it for *any* class, even built-ins). Even if it applies symmetrically to several objects (and there is no "obvious" main target for the call), you can still write it for one of them.

If you really need a "function" that does not apply to an existing object, it sure sounds like a factory function, and it could probably be a special `new` function for the class.

If you need a function that:

- uses no object
- creates no object

Then it's probably that it has strictly only side-effects. Well, either you can just put in an arbitrary class, to "namespace" it (or group it with related functions). Or you can make it a `new` function of a dummy empty class:

	value Print
		new(text t)
			io.print(t)
			return Print	-- default constructor for the "print" class
		end
	end

	-- later
	
	Print("hello")

Semantically: we make a dummy "Print" value, empty, whose constructor has a side effect and then returns a dummy value, that we ignore. Concretely, after optimization: just print "hello".

Heh, look, we just invented global functions. Magic.

One last ambiguity
------------------

Sounds good. I see one last issue worth investigating (that already existed before, mind you). To create an object or a value, the "only" way is the instantiation syntax:

	local foo = Foo(member1 = value1, member2 = value2)

It's the only way in the language to create user-defined objects and values. All members that have default values are optional, so you can sometimes even write:
	foo = Foo()
	foo = Foo

Now unfortunately, this is also the syntax to call the (formerly selfless method) special function `new`, if it has no arguments:

	foo = Foo(a,b,c)
	foo = Foo()
	foo = Foo

Oh noooo. The horror! How do we make the difference? They might be very different:

	object Foo
		num a = 10
		
		new()
			return Foo(a = 40)
		end
	end
	
	foo = Foo()
	assert(foo.a == 10)		-- it was the instantiation syntax
	assert(foo.a == 40)		-- it was the `new` call

Sure, using the `new` only for initial values is dumb, since there is a built-in initial value system. But it was just for the example.

The usual answer, when syntaxes collide, is to change the syntax of one of these. Either the low-level constructor, or the high-level `new` function.

Note that the high-level one is cool, because it gives us this new "global" function trick. And we're used to making objects with a function-like syntax, by passing arguments.

So what if we changed the low-level one, where we list members:

	foo = Foo!(member = value)
	foo = Foo!
	foo = new Foo(member = value)
	foo = new Foo
	foo = @Foo
	foo = $Foo
	foo = &Foo

Running out of tokens. Other brackets? They're all taken:

	foo = Foo[a,b,c,d]		-- array like initialization: foo[1] = a
	foo = Foo{key = value}	-- table-like initialization: foo[key] = value

Using `new` is confusing, since we exactly *not* using the `new` function. Could be something else like `make`, but I don't like reserving too many keywords. The prefixes look weird.

Somehow the `!` is nice. It's imperative: "do it". It emphasizes the instantiation, which is associated with reserving memory (on the heap for objects, maybe on the stack for values). And it's a single character, so it doesn't take too much space if you want to use the member by member syntax by yourself:

	foo = Foo!
	(
		blah = 5,
		thing = "haha"
		etc = false
	)

or even if you want to use all default values (if they exist):

	foo = Foo!

You would write the `new` functions only if you need or want them:

	new(Bar b)
		return Foo!(thebar = b)
	end

For consistency, the compiler could provide a default `new`, if it is possible:

	new()
		return Foo!
	end

What would be the point? Not sure. But I'm thinking it could serve as default constructors do in C++. For instance when you grow a vector of structs ("values"), the constructors are called on the new elements (remember, everything must be initialized). Here we'd need the same:

	local arr = #Foo(20)

Behind the scene, the compiler must initialize all 20 Foos. Should it use the initial values as if it was `Foo!`, or should it call the constructor `new`? If neither exist, of course it must complain.

But which would be more useful? Calling `new` allows the writer of `Foo` total control on the default building of a Foo. Including possible "complex calculations". But by definition `new()` has no argument, so how complex can these computations be?

Remember, we can set initial values for the members:

	value Foo
		num a = 5
		text t = "blah"
		Bar b = Bar(1,"yepee")
	end

and these are used when calling `Foo!`. However these expressions must be somewhat constant: they can't depend on each other. You might want something more complex to happen, even for default cases:

	value Foo
		num a
		text t
		Bar b
		
		new()
			local a = 5
			local t = a.totext
			local b = Bar(a,t)
			
			return Foo!(a = a, t = t, b = b)
		end
	end

It is of course equivalent, but easier to write (and modify) in a single function. It might be a rare case, but then again it's very nice to have the feature if you need it, and it represents no more work in other cases. You can always say that the default compiler-provided `new()` simply calls `Foo!` if it exists.

In any case, of course, if there is no such thing as a default constructor, you must give an initial value to the array resize/create thing:

	local init = Foo(1, "meh", Bar)
	local arr = #Foo(20, init)

Note that this argumentless constructor is also the one that would be called for members in another object/value:

	object Another
		num a		-- implied: "= num()"
		Foo f		-- implied: "= Foo()"
	end
	
	-- since both num and Foo have the "new()" function, we can do:
	local another = Another!
	-- or even:
	local another = Another()

Interestingly, it's a recursion without a base. Built-in types have no members, and therefore using the `!` is useless. It might be allowed anyway, for consistency.

Use in templates
----------------

Consider:

	object Container|(Any T, Any U)
		T t = T()
		#U arr
		
		method resize(uint i)
			arr.resize(i)
		end
		
		method foobar()
			local t,u = T,U
		end
	end

Can you see where I'm going there? Interfaces define only the methods of the objects/values. `new` is not a method. Inside the template, we have no idea if `T()` can be called, or if there is a default value for U in the resizing.

Question:

- do we make `new` part of the interface, so that templates can know what to expect?
- do we delay checking until actual template instantiation?
- do we forbid instantiation in templates?

Number one: impossible. The return type of `new` is the class itself, and it is now notorious that our interfaces can't be self-referential (note that the problem would have been the same with the former selfless factory).

Number two: if we delay this, it's equivalent (and easier) to delay everything, and we fall back to the result that interfaces as bounds are pointless.

Number three: well here we are again. Realizing that templates with bounds have strong constraints. It would seem indeed, that we cannot instantiate the generic types from within templated code. Only use existing ones: move them around, and call the known methods about them.

Is it a problem? I don't know. Containers are supposed to store and retrieve objects made outside. What about simple hash tables, which have lots of "empty" places, for instance:

	value Pair|(Hashable K, Any V)
		K key
		V val
	end
	
	object Table|(Hashable K, Any V)
		#Pair|(K,V) array
		
		new()
			-- make initial free space
			array.resize(8)
		end
	end

Well, this resize on array of values Pair|(K,V) requires that Pair|(K,V) has a constructor without arguments `new()`, which really depends on whether K and V have any. Which we don't know.

Oh wait, not really. This implementation is foolish. All we have to do is use optionals:

	value Pair|(Hashable K, Any V)
		K? key
		V? val
	end

Now the Pair always has a default `new` (which fills nil and nil), and all is well.

	object Table|(Hashable K, Any V)
		#Pair|(K,V) array
		
		V? [](K key)
			
			local pair = array[key.hash % array.size]
			
			if pair.key as key_found then
				if key_found == key then
					return pair.value
				else
					-- lookup the rest of the bin or something
				end
			else
				return nil
			end
		end
		
	end

Where were we already? Ah yes, can't instantiate the template parameter's class within the template. Maybe we don't need to.

Prototype for `new`
-------------------

More problems! Remember how I once complained that without exceptions, you can't properly handle constructor failure? And that using a selfless `new` was nice because it was a perfectly normal method that could return anything you wanted, including an existing object (internizing), nil (failure) or even anything else you wanted?

Could our new `new` do the same? Be declared with any prototype you want, and be just a normal function?

	object Foo
		Foo? new(uint i)
			if i > 5 then
				return nil
			else
				return Foo!
			end
		end
	end

I don't see why not. And that way we can do even more interesting "global function" tricks:

	value give_me_five
		uint new()
			return 5
		end
	end
	
	-- lter
	local meh = give_me_five()

Of course, when the `new()` is used for default values somewhere else, the return type compatibility is checked. But otherwise it poses no problem at all.

Summary of this episode
-----------------------

Initial values for members:

- non optional object members have no default initialization.
- optional object members have the `nil` default initialization.
- value members have the default initialization `new()`, if it exists.
- every member can have an explicit initialization (`= expression`)

Instantiation:

- values and objects are instantiated with the `Foo!(...)` syntax. All members that do not have a default initialization must be provided.
- if the argumentless `Foo!` call is valid, the compiler also provides the default constructor `new()` that simply returns `Foo!`.
- `new()` can be explicitely provided by the user, also with other prototypes.

Arrays:

- growing an array of values is possible only if `new()` exists for that value class, and it is use to create each new value.
- any other case (values without `new()`, objects) requires passing an initial value with which to fill the new elements.

